          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          More sealer is not more protection. Past a certain film thickness the product stops curing properly, and everything that goes wrong afterwards — clouding, tackiness, peeling — traces back to one coat too many or one coat applied too heavy.
        </p>

        <div id="short" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer by Chemistry
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two coats is the default for film-forming sealers and one saturating pass is the default for penetrating ones, but the detail matters because the two are applied in genuinely different ways. A film sealer is spread to a controlled thickness; a penetrating sealer is applied until the slab stops taking it. Confusing those two methods is the single most common application error.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sealer Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Coats</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Method</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Silane-siloxane penetrating</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 saturating, or 2 light wet-on-wet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flood to rejection, spread out any pooling</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">High-solids silane</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 saturating</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Single heavy pass, no ponding</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Silicate densifier</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-2</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Keep wet 15-30 min, then remove residue</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Water-based acrylic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 thin</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cross-rolled, second coat perpendicular</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Solvent-based acrylic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 thin</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cross-rolled or sprayed and back-rolled</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Epoxy garage kit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-2 plus topcoat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Per kit instructions, strict pot life</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Notice that no row says three. There is no common concrete sealer where a third coat is the recommended specification, and there are several where it actively causes failure. When a slab needs more protection than two coats deliver, the answer is different chemistry, not more layers.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other thing the table shows is that coat count and coverage rate are one decision, not two. Two coats at the wrong rate can put less product down than one at the right one. Work out total gallons from the coverage guidance for your product — our guide to <a href='/how-much-concrete-sealer-do-i-need/'>how much concrete sealer you need</a> covers the arithmetic — and then divide that total across the coats.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="penetrating" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Penetrating Sealers: Saturation, Not Thickness
          </h2>

          <p>
            A penetrating sealer works by getting into the pore structure, so the goal is saturation rather than coverage. The standard method is to flood the surface with a low-pressure sprayer until the concrete will not absorb any more, keep it wet for the dwell time the label specifies — usually five to fifteen minutes — and then spread out anything still sitting on the surface with a brush or roller before it dries.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some manufacturers describe this as one coat and others as two light wet-on-wet coats, which sounds like a disagreement but is the same thing. The second pass in a wet-on-wet method goes on within minutes of the first, while the surface is still wet, and its job is to top up the areas that drank the first pass fastest. It is not a separate coat in the sense an acrylic second coat is.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE FOR PENETRATING SEALERS</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Apply until the slab stops absorbing, then remove what is left on top.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Any product still sitting on the surface when it dries has nowhere to go and will cure as a thin, blotchy film that was never intended to exist. Back-roll or broom out puddles within the dwell time and the finish stays invisible, which is what a correctly applied penetrating sealer should be.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            A true second coat on a different day is occasionally specified for very porous concrete or for silane products on vertical surfaces, but it is the exception. If a slab is so absorbent that one saturating pass disappears without wetting out, the more likely issue is that the concrete needs repair rather than more sealer, and our guide to <a href='/sealing-old-concrete/'>sealing old concrete</a> covers what to do about that.
          </p>

          <p>
            The ponding problem is worth stating plainly: excess penetrating sealer left to dry in a low spot produces a white or glossy patch that looks exactly like a defect, because it is one. It can usually be scrubbed off with the matching solvent if caught early, which is why a walk-round with a broom during the dwell time is part of the job rather than a refinement.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="acrylic" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Acrylic Sealers: Two Thin Coats
          </h2>

          <p>
            Film-forming acrylics are formulated to cure at a specific film thickness, and two thin coats reach that thickness with a continuous, well-bonded film. One thick coat containing the same volume of product does not: the surface skins over before the solvent or water underneath has escaped, and the trapped carrier produces cloudiness, tackiness and a film that will lift later.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first coat does most of the sealing work and takes most of the product, because it is going onto bare, absorbent concrete. The second coat covers considerably further — often 30 to 50 percent further — because it lays onto a partially sealed surface. Expecting the second coat to use as much as the first is the usual reason people think they have bought too much.
          </p>

          <p>
            Apply thin enough that the film looks wet but not glossy-deep, and never let product pool in low spots or against edges. On a rolled application that means loading the roller lightly and cross-rolling to even it out. On a sprayed application it means back-rolling behind the spray, which is not optional on a first coat over bare concrete.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want more sheen than two coats deliver, the answer is a higher-solids or gloss-formulated product rather than a third coat of what you have. Sheen comes from the resin content of the sealer, and adding more layers of a satin product produces a thicker satin film rather than a glossy one — along with all the curing problems that thickness brings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="window" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Recoat Window Decides Whether They Bond
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every film-forming sealer has a recoat window: a period after the first coat during which the second will chemically bond into it. Go too early and the second coat's solvent re-dissolves an uncured first coat, producing a soft, wrinkled mess. Go too late and the first coat has fully cured, so the second sits on top as a separate layer that can delaminate.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Recoat Windows</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2-4 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Solvent acrylic, minimum</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">4-8 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Water-based acrylic</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">24 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Practical upper limit</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">0-15 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Penetrating, wet-on-wet</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Those are typical figures and the label always wins, because the window shifts with temperature and humidity. A cool, damp day stretches every number; a hot, dry one compresses them. The practical test for a solvent acrylic is the thumb test: press firmly, and if the film is dry to the touch but still takes a slight print, it is ready for the second coat.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you miss the window entirely — rain arrives, or the job runs into the evening — the safest recovery is to let the first coat cure fully, wait the manufacturer's full cure time, then lightly abrade the surface before recoating. It is more work than doing it on schedule, but it produces a bonded film rather than a layer waiting to peel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rain inside the window is a different problem and usually a worse one. Water landing on a partially cured acrylic blushes the film white and can lift it entirely. The 24-hour rain-free forecast that every label asks for is the real constraint on when this job can be done, not a suggestion — the same lesson covered in our guide to <a href='/rain-after-driveway-sealing/'>rain after driveway sealing</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Why a Third Coat Makes It Worse
          </h2>

          <p>
            Film thickness is the governing constraint on any topical sealer, and the specification exists because of how the film cures and how vapour moves through it. Each additional coat reduces the film's permeability, and concrete slabs — particularly slabs on grade — move moisture vapour upwards continuously. Below a certain permeability that vapour cannot escape, so it collects under the film and pushes it off.
          </p>

          <p>
            The visible result is white blushing, then blisters, then sheets of film lifting away, usually starting in the areas that get least sun. It looks like a product defect and gets blamed on one, but the cause is almost always accumulated thickness from repeated recoating over several years.
          </p>

          <p>
            A thicker film is also more brittle and less able to move with the slab through thermal cycles. Concrete expands and contracts substantially between a summer afternoon and a winter night; a thin film flexes with it, and a heavy built-up one cracks and releases at the edges. That is why the failures show up first at control joints and slab perimeters.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once build-up has occurred, the fix is stripping rather than more product. Chemical stripper, a thorough neutralising wash and a full dry, then two thin coats onto clean concrete. If the idea of periodically stripping a patio is unappealing, that is a good argument for penetrating chemistry on your next reseal — the comparison is laid out in our guide to <a href='/acrylic-vs-penetrating-concrete-sealer/'>acrylic versus penetrating sealers</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="direction" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Technique: Direction, Overlap and Edges
          </h2>

          <p>
            Apply the second coat perpendicular to the first. Cross-rolling covers the thin lines that any roller leaves along its travel direction, and on a large slab it is the difference between a uniform finish and one that shows every pass in low sunlight. It costs nothing and takes no extra time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep a wet edge and work in manageable sections. A driveway sealed in strips that are allowed to dry between them will show lap marks where the sections meet, and those marks are permanent in a film sealer. Plan the sections around control joints where possible, because a joint hides a transition that open concrete would advertise.
          </p>

          <p>
            Work away from your exit. It sounds obvious, and it is still the mistake that strands people in a corner of the garage or at the far end of a driveway with twenty feet of fresh sealer between them and the street. Plan the route before opening the can, and start at the point furthest from where you intend to finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cut the edges in first with a brush on each coat, the way you would paint a room. Rollers cannot reach cleanly against a house wall, a garage threshold or a lawn edge, and trying to make them do so produces either a thin gap or a heavy build-up. A brushed edge of six inches on each coat solves both.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="check" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Checking You Got It Right
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a film sealer, look at the slab in low-angle light the following morning. A correct two-coat application looks uniform in sheen across the whole surface. Patches of higher gloss mean heavier film, usually in low spots, and patches of dull matte mean the film is thin or the concrete absorbed more there. Neither is worth correcting immediately — note them and address them at the next recoat.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a penetrating sealer, the check is water. After the full cure time, which is commonly 24 to 72 hours, pour a cup of water onto several areas. It should bead and sit. Areas where it soaks straight in were either missed or were absorbent enough to need a second application, and they can be re-treated on their own without redoing the slab.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tackiness a day or two after application means too much product, not too little. On a penetrating sealer it means excess dried on the surface; on an acrylic it means the film was applied too thick to cure. Both are usually recoverable with the appropriate solvent and a stiff brush while the problem is fresh, and both become a stripping job if left for a season.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, write down what you used, how many coats and the date, and put it with the leftover product. When the slab needs attention in five years, the difference between knowing what is on it and guessing is the difference between a straightforward refresher coat and a stripping job you did not plan for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">One Thick Coat Instead of Two Thin Ones</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The same volume of product applied in a single heavy pass skins over before the carrier underneath can escape. The result is cloudiness, tackiness and a film that lifts later. Thickness is a specification, not a preference.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Missing the Recoat Window</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Too early and the second coat re-dissolves the first into a wrinkled mess; too late and it sits on top as a separate layer that can delaminate. Check the label's window, adjust for the day's temperature, and use the thumb test on solvent acrylics.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting Penetrating Sealer Dry on the Surface</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Excess product that ponds and dries leaves white or glossy blotches — a film the product was never meant to form. Broom or back-roll puddles out within the dwell time and the finish stays invisible.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Adding a Third Coat for Extra Protection</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Each layer reduces the film's vapour permeability, and slabs on grade push moisture upwards continuously. Past a certain thickness that vapour lifts the film off. More sheen needs a glossier product, not another coat.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Rolling Both Coats in the Same Direction</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Roller travel leaves fine lines that only show in low-angle light, and a second coat laid the same way deepens rather than hides them. Cross-roll the second coat perpendicular to the first.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many coats of concrete sealer should I apply?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Two thin coats for film-forming acrylics, and one saturating pass — or two light wet-on-wet passes — for penetrating silane-siloxane sealers. No common concrete sealer specifies three coats, and on film sealers a third causes vapour to lift the film rather than adding protection.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do penetrating sealers need two coats?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not usually. They are applied to saturation in a single flood pass, kept wet for the label's dwell time, then any surface excess is broomed out. Some manufacturers describe this as two light wet-on-wet coats, which is the same method — the second pass goes on within minutes, not the next day.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long between coats of concrete sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typically two to four hours for solvent acrylics and four to eight for water-based, with 24 hours as a practical upper limit before the first coat cures too hard to bond. Cool damp weather stretches these and hot dry weather shortens them, so follow the label and use the thumb test.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is one coat of concrete sealer enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a penetrating sealer applied to full saturation, yes. For a film-forming acrylic, no — a single coat rarely produces a continuous film, leaving thin areas that fail early. If you only have product for one acrylic coat, buy more before starting rather than stretching what you have.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put a third coat of sealer on concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, but you should not. Each layer reduces vapour permeability, and a slab on grade moves moisture upwards constantly. At a certain accumulated thickness that vapour blisters the film off. If you want more gloss, use a higher-solids product; if you want more protection, change chemistry.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if I apply concrete sealer too thick?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The surface skins over and traps the solvent or water underneath, producing cloudiness, a tacky finish that stays soft for days, and a film that delaminates later. Caught early, the matching solvent and a stiff brush can redistribute it; left to cure, it becomes a stripping job.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should the second coat go on in a different direction?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Cross-rolling the second coat perpendicular to the first covers the fine lines a roller leaves along its travel direction. It costs no extra time and it is the main difference between a finish that looks uniform in low sunlight and one that shows every pass.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

